A NOTE ON THIS FILE, WHICH IS A PLACEHOLDER AND SHOULD BE REPLACED.

Until now this repository carried no licence at all. That is not the same as
being unrestricted: under the Berne Convention an original work is protected
automatically from the moment it is fixed, and the absence of a licence means
nobody has permission to do anything with it. So "all rights reserved" was
already the legal position — this file only states it out loud, and removes the
ambiguity for anyone who reads the repository and wonders.

It therefore forecloses nothing. It is the most conservative option available
and it is deliberately temporary.

The owner intends this work to reach homeschooling families, which the terms
above do not permit. Three replacements were put to the owner:

  1. Keep it locked. These terms, unchanged. Every use approved individually.

  2. Free for families, not for companies. Homeschoolers, co-ops and individual
     teachers may use, copy and share it at no cost; commercial use requires a
     separate agreement. A Creative Commons BY-NC licence is the usual vehicle,
     commonly with ShareAlike or NoDerivatives depending on whether adaptations
     should be permitted.

  3. Fully open. Anyone may use, adapt and sell it, with attribution. MIT or
     Apache-2.0 for the code; CC BY for the curriculum and artwork.

Code and content are often licensed separately, because what suits a program
rarely suits a book. That choice, and the exact legal name of the copyright
holder, are the owner's to make. Nothing here is legal advice.

The name and the licence appear in exactly two places in the running app, both
read from one constant: OWNER and LICENCE in js/screens.js. Changing them there
changes the front door and the Me screen together.
